    "updated_at": "2022-06-28T08:58:30Z",
    "body": "I think it's a decision each feature proposer needs to make for themselves. Pros/cons of HTML pull request:\r\n\r\n- (+) You're more likely to get HTML editor review and thus have spec text which meets the usual standards. (This can avoid errors like proposing a content attribute with dashes in its name, or missing authoring conformance requirements, or using the wrong type of IDL reflection.)\r\n- (+) Less work for your future self in the event of gaining multi-implementer interest: rebasing is usually much easier than converting from Bikeshed/ReSpec into Wattsi, meeting the HTML spec's standards, obsoleting and redirecting the old spec document, etc.\r\n- (+) Raises the visibility to the larger community.\r\n- (-) A PR preview-generated HTML spec can be harder to read for those trying to understand the feature.\r\n- (-) Wattsi is not the best tooling compared to Bikeshed, so if you are rapidly iterating and especially if you might throw away the spec entirely, it could be wasted effort to do the extra work to learn and use Wattsi.\r\n",
